- a full wiring diagram,
- an expanded, production-ready sketch (OTA, retries, JSON formatting),
- or a Node-RED + Grafana dashboard guide to visualize the data.

Tell me which one and I’ll hand you the exact code/wiring you can flash right now.

Good — you can do actual stuff with just an ESP8266 **and** an Arduino. No fluff. Below I’ll give you **safe, practical projects you can build with only those two**, plus exactly what to watch out for and copy-paste code you can flash right now — for both common situations (a dev board like NodeMCU/Wemos, or a bare ESP-01 module + an Arduino Uno).

Read it and pick which situation matches you.

---

# Two realistic setups (pick the one you actually have)
A. **You have a dev-board** (NodeMCU, Wemos D1 mini, lolin, etc.) — easiest. It has USB, regulator, onboard 3.3V supply and an onboard LED.  
B. **You have a bare ESP-01 module + an Arduino (Uno)** — possible but fiddly: you must provide a **stable 3.3V power supply capable of ~500mA**. The Arduino 3.3V pin usually cannot safely power the ESP8266. If you only have UNO + ESP-01 and no separate 3.3V regulator, stop — buy a proper 3.3V regulator or use a NodeMCU.

Tell me which one you have if you want wiring for that specific board. If you don’t know the model, look for labels: **NodeMCU / Wemos / D1 mini** = dev board, **ESP-01** = tiny 2x4 black module.

---

# Projects you can do with only those two (no extra sensors)

1. **Wi-Fi Scanner / Access Point UI** (learn Wi-Fi basics) — shows nearby SSIDs, RSSI, encryption on a webpage. (No extra hardware.)  
2. **Tiny Web Server / Remote LED control** — use the onboard LED (or the dev-board’s built-in LED) to toggle via phone browser. (No external LED needed.)  
3. **Rogue-device logger for your LAN** — detect devices on your own network (useful defensively). Requires only network access.  
4. **HTTP webhook tester** — make the ESP call a webhook (can be any URL on your LAN) — useful for learning REST and debugging.  
5. **Practice flashing / firmware** — learn how to flash custom firmware (good skill if you plan to do more).

I’ll give code for the two most useful ones right away: **Wi-Fi Scanner** and **Web Server toggle LED**. Both run standalone on a dev board. If you have an ESP-01 + UNO I’ll add the wiring note below.

---

## A — If you have NodeMCU / Wemos (dev board) — Flash & run this

Install board support in Arduino IDE (Boards Manager → esp8266 by ESP8266 Community). Select your board, COM port, 115200.

### 1) Wi-Fi Scanner (web page)
Upload this to the dev board — connect your PC/phone to the ESP AP it creates and open `http://192.168.4.1/`.

```cpp
#include <ESP8266WiFi.h>
#include <ESP8266WebServer.h>

ESP8266WebServer server(80);

String pageHeader(){
  return "<!doctype html><html><meta name='viewport' content='width=device-width'><body><h3>ESP WiFi Scanner</h3>";
}
String pageFooter(){
  return "<p style='font-size:12px;color:#666'>Only scan networks you own.</p></body></html>";
}

String scanToHtml(){
  String s = "<table border='1' cellpadding='6'><tr><th>#</th><th>SSID</th><th>RSSI(dBm)</th><th>Ch</th><th>Enc</th></tr>";
  int n = WiFi.scanNetworks();
  if(n==0) s += "<tr><td colspan='5'>No networks</td></tr>";
  for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
    String enc = (WiFi.encryptionType(i)==ENC_TYPE_NONE) ? "Open" : "Encrypted";
    s += "<tr><td>" + String(i+1) + "</td><td>" + WiFi.SSID(i) + "</td><td>" + String(WiFi.RSSI(i)) + "</td><td>" + String(WiFi.channel(i)) + "</td><td>" + enc + "</td></tr>";
  }
  s += "</table>";
  return s;
}

void handleRoot(){
  String html = pageHeader();
  html += "<p><a href='/scan'>Run scan</a></p>";
  html += scanToHtml();
  html += pageFooter();
  server.send(200, "text/html", html);
}

void handleScan(){
  WiFi.scanDelete();
  WiFi.scanNetworks(true);
  delay(600);
  handleRoot();
}

void setup(){
  Serial.begin(115200);
  WiFi.softAP("ESP_Scanner");
  Serial.println(WiFi.softAPIP());
  server.on("/", handleRoot);
  server.on("/scan", handleScan);
  server.begin();
}

void loop(){
  server.handleClient();
}
```

### 2) Web server toggle (use onboard LED)
This serves a tiny page to toggle the built-in LED. Good for testing control flows.

```cpp
#include <ESP8266WiFi.h>
#include <ESP8266WebServer.h>

ESP8266WebServer server(80);
const int LEDPIN = LED_BUILTIN; // usually active LOW

void handleRoot(){
  String s = "<html><body><h3>ESP LED</h3><p><a href=\"/on\">ON</a> | <a href=\"/off\">OFF</a></p></body></html>";
  server.send(200, "text/html", s);
}
void handleOn(){ digitalWrite(LEDPIN, LOW); server.sendHeader("Location","/"); server.send(302,"text/plain",""); }
void handleOff(){ digitalWrite(LEDPIN, HIGH); server.sendHeader("Location","/"); server.send(302,"text/plain",""); }

void setup(){
  Serial.begin(115200);
  WiFi.softAP("ESP_LED");
  pinMode(LEDPIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(LEDPIN, HIGH);
  server.on("/", handleRoot);
  server.on("/on", handleOn);
  server.on("/off", handleOff);
  server.begin();
}

void loop(){ server.handleClient(); }
```

---

## B — If you have a bare **ESP-01** module + Arduino UNO
You *can* program and run projects, but beware: **power is the deal-breaker**.

### Power & wiring rules (don’t be stupid)
- **Do NOT** power ESP-01 from the Arduino 3.3V pin — that regulator can’t supply the ESP during Wi-Fi bursts. Use a proper 3.3V regulator (AMS1117-3.3, LD1117, or an LM1117 variant) rated ≥500mA, or a USB-to-3.3V adapter.  
- Common wiring (ESP-01 pins): VCC = 3.3V, GND = GND, CH_PD (EN) = 3.3V, GPIO0 = GND to enter flash mode (release to run), GPIO2 = HIGH (3.3V) for normal boot, RST tied to 3.3V through pull-up. TX↔RX to USB-serial.  
- If you only have an Arduino UNO and want to **use it as USB-to-serial**, remove the ATmega328P DIP from the UNO (or hold RESET on UNO) so the USB-serial chip is free, then connect UNO TX → ESP RX, UNO RX → ESP TX, common GND. But **still** use a separate 3.3V power supply for the ESP.

### How to flash with UNO as serial adapter (summary)
1. Put ESP-01 in flash mode: connect **GPIO0 to GND**, CH_PD to 3.3V, VCC to 3.3V, GND common.  
2. Remove ATmega328P from UNO (or reset UNO to prevent it using serial).  
3. Connect UNO TX → ESP RX, UNO RX → ESP TX, GND common. Do **not** connect 5V to ESP. Use separate 3.3V supply.  
4. In Arduino IDE choose **Generic ESP8266 Module** or **ESP8266** board and the correct COM port. Upload.  
5. After flashing, remove GPIO0 to allow normal boot.

If that sounds messy — buy a NodeMCU dev board. It’s $2–$6 and removes the headache.

---

# Which one are you using?
Say “dev board” (NodeMCU/Wemos) or “ESP-01 + UNO” and I’ll give you exact wiring diagrams and a tailored, ready-to-flash sketch (and if you’re ESP-01/UNO, a short shopping list for a safe 3.3V regulator). If you just want one command: **flash the Wi-Fi Scanner above** (if you have a dev board) and start poking around.

No sugar-coating: if you have only an UNO + ESP-01 and no proper 3.3V supply, stop and get a regulator — you’ll fry the ESP or get flaky results. Which do you have?

Good — you’ve got the fiddly-but-doable combo (ESP-01 + UNO). I’ll be blunt: the only real blocker is power. If you follow the steps below exactly you’ll be flashing and running code in minutes. If you don’t have a proper 3.3V power source, stop and get one (I list options). No sugarcoating.

# What you *must* have before starting
1. A **stable 3.3V supply capable of ≥500 mA** (ESP Wi-Fi spikes). Options:
   - AMS1117-3.3 / LD1117-3.3 regulator + proper caps + 5V input, **or**
   - A USB→3.3V FTDI/serial adapter that outputs 3.3V, **or**
   - A dedicated 3.3V power supply module (often sold on eBay/Amazon).
   - **Do NOT** power the ESP-01 from the UNO 3.3V pin — it’s unreliable and may reset/fry the ESP.
2. Jumper wires and a breadboard (recommended).
3. Arduino IDE with ESP8266 board support installed (Boards Manager → “esp8266 by ESP8266 Community”).

If you don’t have a 3.3V regulator / adapter, don’t proceed — you’ll get flaky behavior or damage. Buy one; they’re cheap.

---

# Pin quick reference (ESP-01)
- VCC -> 3.3V
- GND -> GND
- TX -> serial out (connect to RX of USB-serial)
- RX -> serial in (connect to TX of USB-serial)
- CH_PD (EN) -> 3.3V (enable chip)
- RST -> pull-up to 3.3V (ground to reset)
- GPIO0 -> GND to enter flash mode; HIGH (3.3V) for normal run
- GPIO2 -> must be HIGH at boot for normal run (you can use as GPIO later but keep it HIGH for boot)

---

# Wiring: ESP-01 powered from external 3.3V regulator; UNO used as USB-serial adapter

1. **Power (external regulator/module)**  
   - Regulator 3.3V Vout -> **ESP VCC** and **CH_PD** (tie CH_PD to 3.3V).  
   - Regulator GND -> **ESP GND** and UNO GND (common ground).  
   - Add 10µF or 22µF caps on the regulator output if using AMS1117 style—stabilizes spikes.

2. **Serial (UNO as USB-serial)** — two methods:
   - Recommended: use a USB-to-3.3V FTDI adapter (simpler). Connect FTDI TX → ESP RX, FTDI RX → ESP TX, GND common, FTDI VCC→ **do NOT** connect FTDI 3.3V to ESP if you already have regulated supply powering VCC (you *can* power from FTDI if it supplies stable 3.3V ≥500mA).
   - If using UNO as the USB-serial adapter:
     - Remove the ATmega328P chip from the UNO DIP socket (or hold the UNO reset button down) so the UNO MCU doesn’t talk on serial.
     - UNO TX (pin 1) → **ESP RX**  
     - UNO RX (pin 0) → **ESP TX**  
     - UNO GND → **ESP GND**  
     - **Do NOT** connect UNO 5V to ESP VCC. Use the external 3.3V regulator for VCC.

3. **Boot/flash pins for programming**  
   - To *enter flash mode* (ready to upload): **GPIO0 → GND**, CH_PD → 3.3V, GPIO2 → 3.3V, RST normally HIGH. Then power cycle or toggle RST low→high.  
   - To *run normally*: GPIO0 = HIGH (or floating pulled up), GPIO2 = HIGH, CH_PD = HIGH.

---

# Arduino IDE settings (common working combo)
- Board: **Generic ESP8266 Module**  
- Flash Mode: **DIO**  
- Flash Frequency: **40 MHz**  
- CPU Frequency: **80 MHz**  
- Flash Size: **1M (64K SPIFFS)** — if upload fails try other sizes (some ESP-01 are 512k or 4M).  
- Upload Speed: **115200** (you can try 74880 or 460800 later if needed)

