Nice catch 👀 — that’s a **C++ string-concatenation error**, not your fault at all.  

Arduino’s `String` class can’t mix raw C-style string literals (`"..."`) with another `"..."` directly — that `"<td>" + "Open" + "</td>"` expression tries to add two `const char*` pointers, which is illegal.  

Let’s fix that cleanly.

---

## 🔧 The Fix
Replace the problematic line inside the function `buildNetworkTable()` with a proper `String()` conversion.

### ❌ Old buggy line:
```cpp
s += "<td>" + (WiFi.encryptionType(i) == WIFI_AUTH_OPEN ? "Open" : "Encrypted") + "</td></tr>";
```

### ✅ Corrected line:
```cpp
s += "<td>" + String(WiFi.encryptionType(i) == WIFI_AUTH_OPEN ? "Open" : "Encrypted") + "</td></tr>";
```

That single `String()` wrapper forces the compiler to treat the text as an Arduino `String`, allowing the `+` concatenation to work.

